.\" Manual page for beebox.
.TH BEEBOX 1 "November 2026" "beebox @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
beebox \- flight recorder that explains why a Linux computer crashed, froze or lost power
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B beebox
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B beebox
keeps a rolling, crash-safe recording of the last minutes of system state:
memory, memory and disk pressure stalls, swap, CPU, temperatures, battery,
free disk space, the applications using the most memory and CPU, and the
kernel's warnings.
When the computer starts after an unexpected end \(em a crash, a freeze that
was reset, a power cut, a battery that ran flat or a sleep it never woke
from \(em
.B beebox
combines that recording with the system journal and any kernel crash log and
writes a report that says, in plain language, what most likely happened and
what to do about it.
.PP
The recorder runs as the
.B beebox.service
systemd unit. Everything else is read-only and can be run by any user.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B last
Show the most recent crash report.
.TP
.BI show " \fR[\fIid\fR]"
Show one report. A unique prefix of the ID is enough.
.TP
.B list
List reports, newest first.
.TP
.BI view " \fR[\fIid\fR]"
Open a report as a web page. The page is self-contained, works offline and
loads nothing from the internet.
.B \-\-sample
shows a sample report.
.TP
.B status
Show whether the recorder is running. Exits with status 3 when it is not.
.TP
.B now
Show the current measurements.
.B \-\-watch
keeps updating.
.TP
.B doctor
Check that the system is set up to catch the next crash and print the exact
command to fix anything that is missing. It changes nothing by itself.
.TP
.B analyze
Analyse a previous boot on demand, from the recording if one exists and from
the journal. Also works for crashes from before
.B beebox
was installed, if the journal kept them. Run with
.B sudo
to read the full kernel log.
.TP
.B record
Run the recorder in the foreground (normally started by systemd).
.TP
.B notify
Show a desktop notification for a report the user has not seen. Started at
login by the XDG autostart entry.
.B \-\-test
shows a sample notification.
.TP
.BI completion " shell"
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ", " zsh " or " fish .
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output (where supported).
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Show every evidence line and finding.
.TP
.BI \-\-html " file"
Write the report as a web page.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
No colour, sparklines or other graphics. Recommended with screen readers.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
No colour.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help for
.B beebox
or a command.
.SH VERDICTS
Each report names a probable cause with a confidence from 0 to 100,
described in words as unclear, possibly, likely or very likely.
Causes include running out of memory, a kernel panic or lockup, a hardware
(machine-check) error, a graphics driver hang, overheating, an empty battery,
never waking from sleep, disk errors, a full disk, disk stalls, a sudden
power loss, and a deliberate SysRq crash. When the evidence does not point
anywhere,
.B beebox
says so rather than guessing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/beebox/beebox.conf
Recorder settings (interval, window, flush, top_processes, keep_reports,
keep_rings). Optional.
.TP
.I /var/lib/beebox/current.ring
The recording for the current boot.
.TP
.I /var/lib/beebox/boots/
Recordings from earlier boots.
.TP
.I /var/lib/beebox/reports/
Crash reports (JSON).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/beebox/
Web pages written by
.BR "beebox view" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour (see https://no-color.org).
.TP
.B BEEBOX_PLAIN
Same as
.BR \-\-plain .
.TP
.B BEEBOX_LOG
Set to
.B debug
for verbose recorder logs.
.TP
.BR BEEBOX_STATE_DIR ", " BEEBOX_RUNTIME_DIR ", " BEEBOX_CONFIG
Override file locations (development and testing).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (or, with
.BR "doctor \-\-strict" ,
something needs fixing).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command or option.
.TP
.B 3
.B status
found that the recorder is not running.
.SH PRIVACY
.B beebox
records only what any local user can already see in
.IR /proc :
process names (never command-line arguments), resource usage and kernel
messages. It never reads file contents and never uses the network; the
service runs with networking disabled. Report evidence has home directory
names, IP addresses and MAC addresses removed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
beebox status
beebox last
beebox view
sudo beebox analyze \-\-boot \-1
beebox show \-\-json > beebox-report.json
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd-oomd (8),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at https://github.com/PurpleDefender249/beebox/issues.
